  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nAn upstream shortwave will track east into the area today continuing \nto provide a steady feed of moist isentropic ascent into a lingering \nwest to east oriented baroclinic zone. This will keep areas of rain, \nlow clouds and drizzle around for most of today into this evening. \nThe saving grace regarding flooding so far has been lacking rainfall \nrates with the main MUCAPE axis remaining off to the southwest. This \nmay change however today given radar trends over northern IL \nassociated with the incoming shortwave that hint at an uptick in \nmore robust rain and chances for embedded thunder, with \nmodel/ob/radar trends pointing to areas mainly along and north of \nthe US 30 corridor this morning for heavier rainfall rates and flood \nimpacts. A slight shift more into the southern half of the forecast \narea then possible this afternoon-evening where differential heating \nnear the sfc portion of the front likely produces some heavier \nconvective elements in areas that cannot take on any more rain. Have \nopted to extend the Flood Watch through 18z today, with further \nextension potentially needed. \n\nThe story then for much of next week will turn to a persistent feed \nof drier, cooler east-northeast flow on the southern fringe of high \npressure laying out across the northern Great Lakes. The main \nmoisture axis shifts south of the area Monday into Tuesday in this \nregime. However, trailing shortwave energy in broad cyclonic flow \naloft may support a few flare ups in shower/drizzle activity, \nespecially on Monday south of US 30.",
